JavaScript and TypeScript are two of the most popular languages for web development, but they serve different roles. TypeScript is a "superset" of JavaScript, meaning it adds an extra layer of features—primarily static typing—while still being able to run any valid JavaScript code. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]  
Key Differences 

• Typing: JavaScript is dynamically typed, where variable types are checked only at runtime. TypeScript uses static typing, catching type-related errors during development. 
• Compilation: JavaScript is an interpreted language that browsers run directly. TypeScript must be compiled (transpiled) into JavaScript before a browser can execute it. 
• Features: TypeScript introduces advanced concepts like Interfaces, Enums, Generics, and Decorators that are not natively part of standard JavaScript. 
• IDE Support: Because of its type system, TypeScript offers superior autocompletion, refactoring, and navigation in code editors like  Visual Studio Code 
. 

